<h2>The statutory base: three provisions working together</h2>
<p>Three provisions make the rented matrimonial home a protected space. Section 2(s) brings tenanted premises within the definition of shared household. Section 17 declares that every woman in a domestic relationship has the right to reside in the shared household, whether or not she has any right, title or beneficial interest in it — language that fits a tenancy precisely, since even the husband-tenant holds no title, only a contractual right. Section 19 then supplies the operative orders: restraining dispossession, restraining the respondent from entering portions of the home, restraining alienation or renunciation of rights in the household, and — crucially for tenancies — directing the respondent to secure the same level of alternate accommodation or pay rent for it.</p>

<h2>What a residence order can and cannot do in a tenancy</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><strong>It can</strong><p>Restrain the respondent from dispossessing the aggrieved person; restrain him from surrendering the tenancy or colluding to end it; direct him to keep paying rent; direct alternate accommodation of the same standard if the home cannot be continued.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><strong>It cannot</strong><p>Create a tenancy between the aggrieved person and the landlord; compel the landlord to renew an expired lease; defeat a genuine eviction obtained by the landlord through due process; or convert a time-bound contractual right into a permanent roof.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The distinction matters because the "renunciation" clause in Section 19 is tailor-made for tenancies. An owner-respondent defeats a residence order by selling; a tenant-respondent defeats it more cheaply — he surrenders the lease, stops paying rent, or quietly tells the landlord to start eviction. Each of those moves can be restrained or neutralised, but only if the order is drafted to anticipate them.</p>

<h2>The landlord\'s position</h2>
<p>The landlord is not a respondent in the domestic relationship and the Act does not strip his proprietary rights. His lease is with the tenant; when it expires or rent stops, his remedy of eviction through due process survives. The friction arises in two situations. The first is collusion — a respondent who procures a friendly eviction to accomplish indirectly what the residence order forbids directly. Courts look at timing, the relationship between landlord and respondent, and whether market behaviour (notice, arrears demands, alternative lettings) is genuine. The second is the landlord\'s own uncertainty: faced with a DV order he is not party to, he often freezes. The clean solution for an aggrieved person is to seek orders that route the obligation through the respondent — continued rent payment or substituted accommodation — rather than orders purporting to bind the owner.</p>

<h2>Practical sequencing for counsel</h2>
<p>For the aggrieved person: obtain the lease and rent receipts early; plead the tenancy expiry date; seek an ad-interim restraint on surrender of the tenancy under Section 23 the day the application is filed, because surrender takes one letter; and always plead Section 19(1)(f) in the alternative so the relief survives the lease. For respondents: the honest defences are about the household\'s character — that the premises were never a shared household, or that the tenancy ended by efflux of time without any act of his — not self-created arrears. And for both sides, remember that monetary relief under Section 20 includes accommodation costs, so the economics of the dispute can usually be resolved even where the particular flat cannot be saved.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This is a general explainer on the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 as it applies to tenanted shared households, and is not legal advice. Outcomes depend on the lease terms, the conduct of the parties and the evidence in each case.</p>
</div>
